## Facts and protocol shapes, no copied implementation

These contributed facts this project encodes — model ids, context windows, effort-level
names, provider endpoints, protocol formats. Facts are not copyrightable and implementing a
published protocol is not copying an implementation of it. Credited in the acknowledgements
list in `README.md`.

| Project | Licence |
| --- | --- |
| models.dev | MIT |
| 9router | MIT |
| trae-mate | MIT |
| dsh-cline-pass | MIT |

## Historical implementation sources — licence texts not bundled here

Three projects informed the free lane's request shaping before it was rewritten. None of
their code is vendored here, and the implementations that remain have since been rewritten
against the wire behaviour rather than against their source. What survives is behaviour and
vocabulary, not text.

| Project | Licence | What remains |
| --- | --- | --- |
| opencode2api | **GPL-3.0** | Nothing textual. `src/o2a/toolshape.js` and `src/o2a/tool-renames.js` were rewritten; no function name, constant list or branch order is shared with `internal/gateway/toolshape.go` or `internal/protocol/tools.go`. The behaviour they implement — how a caller's tool surface is reshaped into the official one — was measured off the wire. The tool descriptions in `src/o2a/assets/ofm-tool-contracts.json` do overlap with `internal/gateway/opencode_tools.json`, but that is a shared ancestor, not a copy: both are generated from `sst/opencode` v2.2.3 (MIT), and this repository's copy is generated from that tag by `scripts/build-o2a-assets.mjs`. |
| free-claude-code | **AGPL-3.0-only** | `src/local-optimize.js` was rewritten: the five request shapes are now a table of detectors with a documented evaluation order, and the ids (`quota`/`prefix`/`title`/`suggestion`/`filepath`) survive only because they are persisted configuration keys — renaming one would silently re-enable an optimization a user had switched off. `src/providers/registry.json` keeps 60 of its 61 historical provider ids for the same reason: they are user-visible config keys, and `docs/provider-compatibility.md` records the compatibility constraint. The registry content itself is rebuilt from models.dev and each entry's `notes` field says so. |
| RTK | Apache-2.0 | The `src/token-saver/compact/` filters were rewritten. They still emit RTK's output markers and use its thresholds, because those markers are a wire format a model has been trained to read — changing them would change model behaviour, not make the code more original. The noise-directory list has the same membership but is regrouped by category rather than kept in upstream order. Full text above. |

**This file still does not ship the GPL-3.0 or AGPL-3.0-only texts, and that is a deliberate
position, not an oversight.** Shipping a licence text alongside code is not what creates or
discharges a copyleft obligation — the obligation attaches to the code, and the question is
whether any code here is a derivative work of those projects. After the rewrites there is no
shared text and no shared identifier vocabulary left in either area; what remains is protocol
behaviour, which is not copyrightable, and persisted configuration keys, which are not
expression either. That is the project's reading, and it is recorded here rather than
presented as a settled legal conclusion.

The project's own verification notes say the same thing: `src/o2a/SYNC.md` records that the
historical source was read and that a clean-room claim cannot be made — the rewrites were
written by people who had read the prior implementations, which is why this section states
the relationship plainly instead of claiming independence from the prior reading.
